- **Round 364: the printer writes as print.c's printchar and strout do.**  print.c writes while it walks: printchar one character and strout one block, into print_buffer (a buffer or marker; print_finish inserts it), onto stdout in a batch session (an fwrite each), into the echo area and the message log in an interactive one, or to a function called with each character.  Emaxx built an object's text whole and handed it over at the end (round 363's recorded finding).  print/output.rs's PrintOutput now remembers the strouts' blocks (and which are a C string's octets, written to a stdio stream as they were), that strout was called at all, and every other character as a printchar's (`pieces', `last_printchar').  print.rs has PrintSink (Buffer, Stdout, Echo, Function: printcharfun as print_prepare leaves it), `deliver' (printchar and strout of each piece onto a sink), `printchar', printchar_unsigned (Fwrite_char's), `print_string' and print_string_to_function (the string read again for each character, to the sizes read at the start), and with_print_buffer (print_buffer as print_prepare empties it and as print_unwind or print_free_buffer leave it).  print/object.rs's Printer writes where print.c does: `out' is print_buffer itself for a buffer sink (`held': taken from PrintGlobals.buffer, handed back by `flush' before Lisp runs and taken again by `resume'), nothing for a function (printchar_code calls it), up to STDOUT_PENDING (4096) bytes for stdout, whose stream makes the same writes of the same fwrites whenever they come, and the echo area's text until the next flush; `flush' runs before `print-unreadable-function', at the end and on an error return (print() flushes before it reports the walk's error, so an error of the output's own comes first).  The print variables are read as print.c reads them, each where the printer first needs it and again after Lisp has run: `vars' reads those not yet read, `lisp_ran' forgets them all, and print() itself reads only `print-continuous-numbering' and `print-circle'; what print.c writes before it reads is written first (a list's parenthesis, a quote, `#<buffer ', a label's `#N=', `#s(hash-table', a string's text before its intervals), and a symbol's name is read for each character.  The pieces are print.c's: a fixnum one strout, nil and t printchars, octalout printchars, `\x' escapes and `\ ' strouts, `#<subr NAME>' two print_c_strings and a printchar, frame, window, process, thread, mutex and condition variable names through print_string, print_bool_vector's pieces, a string found in `print-number-table' a strout of its data (strout_string), a string printed by `prin1' to a function a character at a time from print_prune_string_charset's copy (print_pruned_string says when it is the string itself) with the escape variables read for each (prin1_string_char is one turn of that loop).  print.c's statics are one each: PRSTACK is the print stack of every print (thread-local, as round 348's RDSTACK; each print works above its base_sp and a non-local exit leaves its entries, which the print it was run from then finishes: GNU's `(a b z) c)'), and PrintGlobals.buffer is print_buffer (a nested print into a buffer saves the outer text and print_unwind's copy of SCHARS bytes leaves `pos' where the inner print ended: PrintOutput::overwritten_by, GNU's "(1zz 3)").  file_io.rs: with_print_stream (print_prepared) is print_prepare and print_finish around a body given the sink; print_finish_insertion runs inside print_buffer's extent, in the buffer current then: hash_insert.rs's insert_print_buffer is insert_1_both with PREPARE (no properties inherited, no update_compositions) and `signal_after_change (PT - pos, 0, pos)', the text and `pos' read after prepare_to_modify_buffer (print_finish_text), so a print a before-change hook runs into a buffer changes both, as in GNU; PrintGlobals.inserting counts the insertions waiting on their hooks and PrintGlobals.memory is print_buffer's bytes past `pos_byte' while one waits (exact for texts whose bytes are characters; where a copy ends inside a multibyte character each stray byte stays a raw byte).  A print of no characters is the signal alone.  select_printer_destination sets the marker printed to in the buffer current after the insertion and gives the out-of-range error its datum; with_printer_destination restores the buffer current at print_prepare for every sink.  print_object_to_stream is Fprin1, Fprinc and Fprint (newlines as printchars), write_printer_output is write_string, write_c_string the same for a Lisp string's SSDATA (to its first NUL, a function handed each byte as a C char, the echo area a character a byte and the log the text), printchar_to_stream takes stdout or stderr and any unsigned int; the guesses record_batch_standard_output_char and native_print_updates_batch_last_char are gone, `printchar_stdout_last' being the last character `deliver' saw printchar write.  batch_stdout.rs: write_chars is an fwrite a character (Stream::xsputn_chars).  display.rs: print_error_message (data, stream, context, caller) is one function, used by Ferror_message_string with the hidden buffer and by Fcommand_error_default_function with `external-debugging-output' and Fterpri in a batch session and t in an interactive one; `terpri' is Fterpri (ENSURE: an error for a function, `printchar_stdout_last' in a batch session for any sink but a buffer, the echo area's text for the echo area, Fbolp otherwise); `write-char' printchar in its own print_prepare; `princ' has Fprinc's fast path; `print-level' is any fixnum; `current-message' is nil while the echo area's text is empty.  ccl.rs's written_character gives a negative int's low byte as the ASCII character it is.  The grouped gate passed its GATE_COUNT library tests (a_function_stream_is_called_as_the_printer_walks, prints_into_buffers_share_print_buffer, a_print_goes_on_from_what_lisp_left_it and two of the stream are new); the integration targets (cli, cli_parity with printed_text_reaches_its_output_as_the_walk_goes, a_print_into_a_buffer_inside_another_shares_the_print_buffer, terpri_asks_what_printchar_last_wrote, the_standard_display_table_shows_what_goes_to_stdout, a_batch_error_is_printed_onto_the_debugging_output, a_print_of_nothing_still_owes_the_batch_newline, printing_to_t_fills_the_echo_area_and_the_message_log, a_print_copies_its_text_once_the_change_hooks_have_run, a_code_that_is_no_character_is_written_as_char_string_stores_it, a_print_ends_in_the_buffer_it_began_in and stdout_is_written_a_character_and_a_block_at_a_time new, ert_runner, native_comp_identity, native_thread_continuations, package_lifecycle, runtime_ownership) passed, and clippy is clean.  PROBES364  REVIEW364  tools/round_audit.py AUDIT364
